FIR Advanced Channel Mode
The AMD Vivado™ FIR IP supports time-division-multiplexed (TDM) interleaved channels in two
modes, selected by chan_seq in the configuration struct:
Basic mode (chan_seq = hls::ip_fir::basic): all channels operate in round-robin order;
input samples are distributed one by one across channels.
Advanced mode (chan_seq = hls::ip_fir::advanced): the distribution sequence is pre-
defined and user-selectable at runtime. Different channels can receive non-uniform numbers of
input samples per group.
Configuring advanced channel mode. The static parameter sequence_list defines the set of pre-
defined patterns that can be selected at runtime. For example, a 4-channel FIR with all five
4-channel patterns available:
struct myconfig : hls::ip_fir::params_t {
static const unsigned num_channels = 4;
static const unsigned input_length = INPUT_LENGTH / 4;
static const unsigned output_length = OUTPUT_LENGTH / 4;
static const unsigned chan_seq = hls::ip_fir::advanced;
static const inline hls::ip_fir::chan_sequence_pattern sequence_list[5]
= {
hls::ip_fir::P4_0, hls::ip_fir::P4_1,
hls::ip_fir::P4_2, hls::ip_fir::P4_3, hls::ip_fir::P4_4
};
// ... coefficients and other parameters
};
In advanced mode, the run() method takes a per-channel config array. Two helper methods
are provided to populate it without manually handling bit-width alignment: // Sets the channel
sequence pattern index (index into sequence_list) void setChanSequence(unsigned sequence_idx,
config_t& base);
// Sets the channel sequence pattern index (index into sequence_list)
void setChanSequence(unsigned sequence_idx, config_t& base);
// Sets the coefficient set index for a channel
void setCoeffSet(unsigned set_idx, config_t& base);
